**The driver’s-bag link is stronger; the card’s holder, actual visit and connection to a moving job remain unverified.**

- **Receipt linkage:** The police report explicitly references an attached FBI receipt for the driver’s black bag. Its separate white-sack/$4,700 description matches Section 2 p.80, strengthening the inference that **p.81 belongs to the driver’s bag**. A clearer police copy names the driver as Sivan Kurzberg, but omits the receipt attachments. This supports an association—not a documented identification of the cardholder. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}
- **Custody:** [Section 5 p.132](https://themoreuknow.com/pdf/fbi%20report%20section%205.pdf#page=132) supplies the crosswalk **1B4/E01889578 ↔ 1B25/E01889034**; p.136 contains a separate transfer request. Neither allocates the card. The [February 18, 2005 memo](https://themoreuknow.com/pdf/fbi%20report%20section%206.pdf#page=89) says abandoned property **will be destroyed**, without documenting completed destruction or identifying this card.
- **Access comparisons:** NIST received PA authorization lists, and a later paper reports a May 2001 access count. The lists are not entry logs; the count could derive from an aggregate report. Neither establishes coverage of this visitor card or present-day retention. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"}

**The strongest unresolved target is the complete CSRR 014157 attachment packet and the FD-597-to-property-register crosswalk.** No card image, authenticated entry, job match, or explicit positive or negative FBI comparison with a WTC register was located. That is a bounded search result, not proof no inquiry occurred.

[:chatgpt-content-reference{index="3"}](https://chatgpt.com/library?file_id=libfile_13f432dd7cf081919e915c8d0ad5ed7e&account_id=personal) contains the dated provenance, exact pages, custody discrepancies, ordinary alternatives and prioritized custodians. No contacts or requests were made. A confirmed move would not establish explosives placement.

**The physical mover remains unidentified. I found a significant access-related record, a direct Hunter–Zim client connection, and more precise archival targets.**

- **Access correction:** A September 11, 2001 FBI property receipt lists a **WTC visitors card** among a fanny pack’s contents (**Section 2, PDF p.81**). The building numeral is ambiguous between **1 and 7**, the identifying field is redacted, and the card’s date is clipped. “Received from” is checked. This establishes a recorded object; its holder, use, destination floor and Zim connection remain unverified. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="0"}

- **Insurance comparison:** The August 10 fax (**p.13; duplicate p.32**) supplies UMS/Max carrier identifiers without identifying a Zim job. The DeBellis interview (**pp.1–3**) references **NK1765/1A**, and substantial insurance paperwork follows at **pp.4–66**. The unresolved question is attachment **completeness and provenance**; describing the entire packet as unreleased would be inaccurate. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="1"}

- **Hunter connection:** [Hunter Realty’s client list captured March 29, 2003](https://web.archive.org/web/20030329104101if_/http://www.hunterorganization.com/rclient.html) names Zim. It corroborates the business relationship but supplies no project address, moving carrier, invoice or subcontractor.

The strongest new archive targets are **Library of Virginia accession 40168, Box 2/Folder 15**, containing the cataloged Zim incentive-project file, and **New York Historical MS 3128, Box 5/Folder 12**, cataloging a July 16, 2001 WTC lease. Neither folder’s contents were inspected. :chatgpt-content-reference{index="2"}

No Zim vendor award, paid moving invoice, bill of lading, subcontractor list or actual dock/elevator/access log was recovered—for UMS, Max or another carrier. **That is a bounded negative search, not proof no job occurred.** No custodians were contacted. A move would not establish explosives installation.

